.TH UDPCHAINS 1 "2026-10-21" "udpchains 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
udpchains \- route a program's UDP traffic through a SOCKS5 proxy or a chain of them
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B udpchains
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIprogram\fR [\fIargs\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B udpchains
runs
.I program
with an
.BR LD_PRELOAD (8)
shim that redirects its
.B UDP
traffic through a SOCKS5 proxy supporting
.BR "UDP ASSOCIATE" " (RFC 1928 \[sc]7),"
or through a chain of up to 8 of them.
The program is not modified or recompiled.
See
.BR CHAINS .
.PP
Unlike
.BR proxychains (1),
which is TCP\-only and lets UDP go direct with no error,
.B udpchains
.B fails closed:
if a datagram cannot be proxied the call fails with an appropriate
.IR errno .
It never falls back to sending directly.
.PP
A statically linked program, a Go program, or one making system calls without
libc cannot be helped by a shim.
.B udpchains \-\-supervise
runs such a program under a supervisor instead, which carries its UDP from
outside it.
See
.BR "SUPERVISOR MODE" .
.PP
There is no built\-in default proxy. If none is configured,
.B udpchains
prints a diagnostic and refuses to run the program rather than running it
unproxied.
.PP
There is no default DNS resolver either.
Name lookups are sent through the proxy to the resolver named by
.BR UDPCHAINS_DNS ;
without one the program still runs, but any lookup that needs DNS fails.
See
.BR "NAME RESOLUTION" .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Configuration file.
.TP
.BI \-p " addr"
Proxy address, as \fIhost:port\fR or \fI[v6]:port\fR.
Repeat it for a chain: the first is hop 1.
Given on the command line, it replaces any chain from the environment or the
configuration file.
.TP
.B \-\-supervise
Run the program in supervisor mode instead of with the shim.
See
.BR "SUPERVISOR MODE" .
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: errors only.
.TP
.B \-v
Verbose: log protocol steps. Never logs payload bytes.
.TP
.B \-V
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h
Show help.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
Configuration is resolved in precedence order: command line, environment,
configuration file.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_PROXY
Proxy as \fIhost:port\fR, or a chain as a comma\-separated list of them.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_CONFIG
Configuration file path.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_MAX_DATAGRAM
Maximum payload in bytes. Default 8192. Payloads above this fail with
.BR EMSGSIZE .
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_STRICT_LOOPBACK
Promote the loopback\-destination warning to an error.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_CONNECT_TIMEOUT
Timeout in milliseconds for each step of the proxy handshake, not for the
whole of it.
Default 5000.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_CHAIN_TIMEOUT
Timeout in milliseconds for the whole of an association's setup, across every
hop, from 0 to 4800000.
Unset, it is
.B UDPCHAINS_CONNECT_TIMEOUT
times the number of hops for a chain, and there is none for a single proxy.
Set, it applies to a single proxy too.
0 means no limit.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS
Resolver for name lookups, as a numeric \fIip\fR, \fIip:port\fR or
\fI[v6]:port\fR (default port 53).
Queries to it are sent through the proxy.
There is no default; see
.BR "NAME RESOLUTION" .
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS_TIMEOUT
Timeout for a whole name lookup, in milliseconds.
Default 5000.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS_SEARCH
Search domains, separated by spaces or commas, tried for a name with no dot.
None by default:
.I /etc/resolv.conf
is not read.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_HOSTS_FILE
Hosts file consulted before DNS.
Default
.IR /etc/hosts .
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_LIB
Explicit path to \fIlibudpchains.so\fR.
.TP
.BR UDPCHAINS_USERNAME ", " UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_FILE
Credentials for RFC 1929 username/password authentication. Never accepted on
the command line, because
.I argv
is visible in
.BR ps (1).
Method
.B 0x02
is offered only when credentials are configured. GSSAPI is not implemented.
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD
The password itself.
Everything the program starts inherits it; prefer
.BR UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_FILE .
.TP
.BR UDPCHAINS_USERNAME_ \fIn\fR ", " UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_FILE_ \fIn\fR ", " UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_ \fIn\fR
Hop \fIn\fR's credentials, for \fIn\fR from 2.
Hop 1 uses the names without a suffix.
.TP
.BR UDPCHAINS_QUIET ", " UDPCHAINS_DEBUG
As
.B \-q
and
.BR \-v .
Each takes a boolean:
.BR 1 / 0 ,
.BR yes / no ,
.BR true / false
or
.BR on / off .
.TP
.B UDPCHAINS_SUPERVISE
A boolean, as
.BR \-\-supervise .
It is not passed to the program.
.PP
.B udpchains
sets
.BR UDPCHAINS_RESOLVED ,
.B UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_CONFIG
and
.BI UDPCHAINS_PASSWORD_CONFIG_ n
for the program it runs.
They are not meant to be set by hand.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.udpchains.conf
.TQ
.I /etc/udpchains.conf
Configuration, as \fIkey = value\fR lines.
The first of
.BR \-f ,
.BR UDPCHAINS_CONFIG ,
.I ~/.udpchains.conf
and
.I /etc/udpchains.conf
that exists is read, and no other.
.IP
Keys:
.BR proxy ,
.BR max_datagram ,
.BR strict_loopback ,
.BR connect_timeout ,
.BR chain_timeout ,
.BR dns ,
.BR dns_timeout ,
.BR dns_search ,
.BR hosts_file ,
.BR username ,
.B password_file
(relative to the current directory),
.B password
(inline; the file must then not be readable by others),
.BR quiet ,
.B debug
and
.BR supervise .
Each matches the environment variable of the same name.
An unknown key is an error.
.IP
Each
.B [hop]
section adds the next hop of a chain after the top\-level
.BR proxy ,
and may hold only
.BR proxy ,
.BR username ,
.B password_file
and
.BR password .
.IP
A
.I proxychains.conf
can be given to
.B \-f
as it is.
Each
.B socks5
line of its
.B [ProxyList]
is a hop, with its username and password if it has them;
.BR socks4 ,
.B http
and
.B raw
lines are errors, since they cannot carry UDP.
.B strict_chain
is accepted, and
.BR dynamic_chain ,
.B random_chain
and
.B round_robin_chain
are errors.
.B quiet_mode
and
.B tcp_connect_time_out
set
.B quiet
and
.BR connect_timeout .
Its other directives, such as
.B proxy_dns
and
.BR localnet ,
are not applied, and each is named in a warning.
A file has either a
.B [ProxyList]
or
.B proxy
and
.B [hop]
sections, not both.
.IP
Comments are whole lines only, beginning with
.B #
or
.BR ; .
A
.B #
after a value, following whitespace, is an error rather than part of the
value; a
.B #
inside a value is kept.
.SH NAME RESOLUTION
glibc's resolver makes its socket calls through internal functions that no
preloaded library can see, so
.B udpchains
answers
.BR getaddrinfo (3),
.BR getnameinfo (3),
.BR gethostbyname (3)
and the
.BR res_query (3)
family itself.
A numeric address answers itself;
.I localhost
and names under it are loopback; then the hosts file; then a DNS query to the
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS
resolver, sent through the proxy.
An answer too large for UDP is repeated over TCP with SOCKS5 CONNECT.
.PP
The host's resolver is never used.
Without a configured resolver a lookup that needs DNS fails with
.BR EAI_FAIL ;
with an unreachable proxy it fails with
.BR EAI_AGAIN .
Other name services in
.BR nsswitch.conf (5),
such as mDNS and
.BR systemd-resolved (8),
are skipped.
Non-ASCII names and
.BR getaddrinfo_a (3)
are refused.
.PP
Programs with their own resolver, such as
.BR dig (1),
send ordinary UDP to the nameserver in
.IR /etc/resolv.conf ,
which is proxied like any other datagram.
.SH CHAINS
A chain sends each datagram through every proxy listed, in order.
Hop 1 is the one this host talks to; the last, the exit hop, sends the
datagram to its destination.
Each hop after the first is reached by SOCKS5 CONNECT through the hops before
it, so every hop but the exit must allow CONNECT to the next one, and must
relay UDP to it.
.PP
Chains are strict: if any hop cannot be reached, negotiated or associated,
the call fails, and the diagnostic names the hop.
No hop is skipped.
Hops after the first are never resolved on this host: a name is sent to the
hop before it, which resolves it.
Name lookups through
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS
use the chain too.
A loopback destination is resolved at the exit hop, so it always warns.
.PP
Each hop's buffer must hold the headers of the hops after it as well as the
payload, so a hop that is not the exit truncates sooner than it would alone:
hop \fIi\fR of \fIn\fR, 10 bytes sooner for each later hop with an IPv4
relay, 22 for IPv6.
Set
.B max_datagram
no higher than the smallest such limit.
.PP
The shim holds a TCP connection per hop for every socket.
When its protected descriptor range (768 and up) is full, a connection stays
at a lower number, still protected, and a warning is printed; a
.BR dup2 (2)
onto that number then fails with
.BR EBUSY .
.PP
Chains add no encryption.
Every hop can read every datagram, and hop \fIk\fR's credentials pass through
hops 1 to \fIk\fR\-1 in cleartext.
.SH SUPERVISOR MODE
With
.BR \-\-supervise ,
.B supervise = yes
or
.BR UDPCHAINS_SUPERVISE=1 ,
.B udpchains
stays running as the program's parent, the supervisor.
The program runs under a
.BR seccomp (2)
filter, and the supervisor, attached as its tracer with
.BR ptrace (2),
handles each system call the filter selects while the calling thread is
stopped: it carries the program's UDP through the proxy or chain, lets other
calls run, or fails them with an
.IR errno .
Nothing changes in the program, so statically linked programs, Go programs and
programs making system calls themselves are carried.
The program is never given the shim.
It is never chosen automatically.
.PP
It needs Linux 5.14 or later on x86\-64, a
.I /proc/sys/kernel/yama/ptrace_scope
of 1 or less, permission to install a seccomp filter and to trace (some
container runtimes forbid one or the other), and a program that is not already
being traced.
Otherwise
.B udpchains
refuses to run the program; it never runs it unsupervised.
If the supervisor is killed, so is the program.
.PP
.B The program's UDP sockets live at descriptors 768 and up
(lower if the file limit is under 1024), where the supervisor places them.
A UDP socket among the descriptors the program would inherit makes
.B udpchains
refuse to start.
.PP
.B Name lookups.
The program runs its own resolver, which reads
.IR /etc/resolv.conf .
A query to port 53 of the system resolver \- a loopback address, or a
nameserver listed in
.IR /etc/resolv.conf ,
read again when it changes \- is sent through the proxy to
.B dns
instead, and its reply reported as from the address asked.
Without
.BR dns ,
such a query fails with
.BR ENETUNREACH .
Port 53 anywhere else is proxied as addressed.
A TCP connection to the system resolver's port 53 is refused with
.BR ECONNREFUSED ,
so a truncated answer fails rather than reaching the resolver around the
proxy.
.PP
.B Refused:
.BR io_uring_setup (2)
.RB ( ENOSYS ),
.BR pidfd_getfd (2)
.RB ( EPERM ),
raw and packet sockets
.RB ( EPERM ),
ICMP and UDP\-Lite sockets
.RB ( EPROTONOSUPPORT ),
.BR sendfile (2)
and
.BR splice (2)
onto a UDP socket
.RB ( EINVAL ),
and a send or connect on a UDP socket the supervisor did not make, received
from another process
.RB ( EPERM ).
.PP
.B Limitations.
It is not a sandbox: a call let through after a check can be changed by the
program in between, from another thread, so it keeps a cooperative program
from leaking by accident but does not contain a hostile one.
A UDP socket received from an unsupervised process already connected, then
written with
.BR write (2),
.BR writev (2)
or
.BR pwritev2 (2),
is sent directly.
The program cannot be debugged with
.BR gdb (1)
or traced with
.BR strace (1)
from inside the supervision, and one that uses
.BR ptrace (2)
itself gets
.BR EPERM ;
.B udpchains
itself under
.B strace \-f
refuses to start.
A program built with LeakSanitizer cannot run its leak check.
A process that makes itself non\-dumpable, such as
.BR ssh\-agent (1),
cannot be inspected, and its connects and sends fail with
.BR EPERM .
Before Linux 6.9, a thread that has unshared its descriptor table is refused
its UDP calls.
Every send and connect, UDP or not, and every call on a UDP socket costs a
round trip to the supervisor, of tens of microseconds.
While an association is set up, the program's other supervised calls wait.
A blocking
.BR recvmmsg (2)
returns the datagrams already queued, at least one, rather than waiting until
it has as many as asked.
A signal arriving while an association is set up is handled at once, and the
call made again.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The program ran and exited successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Configuration, proxy or shim\-loading failure. The program was not run.
.TP
.B 126
The program could not be executed.
.TP
.B 127
The program was not found.
.SH SECURITY
.B udpchains
keeps a program's UDP from bypassing the proxy.
It does not make the path to the proxy private.
.PP
RFC 1929 credentials cross the network in cleartext.
Datagrams to and from the proxy's relay are neither authenticated nor
encrypted: anyone on that path can read them, and a packet forged to appear
to come from the relay can claim any source address.
Reach a remote proxy over a VPN you trust, or use one on this host; an SSH
port forward carries the control connection but not the UDP relay traffic.
.PP
Report vulnerabilities privately, as described in
.IR SECURITY.md .
.SH LIMITATIONS
Without
.BR \-\-supervise ,
cannot help statically\-linked binaries, Go binaries, or programs making
system calls without libc: there is no libc call to interpose on.
Calls made through libc's
.BR syscall (2)
are routed to the hooks. These limitations are the shim's; supervisor mode
has its own, under
.BR "SUPERVISOR MODE" .
Refuses setuid, setgid and
capability\-endowed binaries, because the loader ignores
.B LD_PRELOAD
for them and running them would mean running them unproxied.
.PP
.B Raw and packet sockets are refused
with
.BR EPERM ,
as they are without
.BR CAP_NET_RAW .
.PP
.B io_uring
sends go straight to the kernel and bypass the shim.
On a UDP socket,
.BR sendfile (2),
.BR splice (2)
and
.BR fdopen (3)
are refused with
.BR EINVAL ;
.BR dprintf (3)
and
.BR pwritev2 (2)
are proxied, and a socket copied with
.BR pidfd_getfd (2)
is tracked. See
.I DECISIONS.md
D\-36.
.PP
TCP is not proxied. Multicast and broadcast fail with
.BR ENETUNREACH .
A loopback destination is resolved at the proxy, so with a remote proxy it
reaches the proxy host's loopback, not this one.
.PP
Ancillary data
.RB ( msg_control )
is returned empty.
.PP
UDP servers do not work: a server's socket is pointed at the proxy's relay,
which its clients cannot reach.
.PP
.B MSG_MORE
and a
.B UDP_SEGMENT
control message are refused with
.BR EOPNOTSUPP .
.BR UDP_CORK ,
.B UDP_SEGMENT
and
.B UDP_GRO
set with
.BR setsockopt (2)
corrupt data rather than failing.
The first send or receive on a socket blocks while the proxy handshake runs,
even on a non\-blocking socket.
.B strict_loopback
is checked only when a socket's association is set up, and only for a
connected socket.
A socket shared with another process is re\-pointed for both.
.PP
After
.BR fork (2),
a child using an inherited socket is given a private one; if the parent still
holds its bound port, the child sends from another port.
A proxy given by hostname is reached at its first address only.
A non\-NULL invalid pointer inside a message faults in the shim instead of
returning
.BR EFAULT .
.PP
Sending from a signal handler is safe only on a socket already in use.
.PP
Tested against real proxies on the same host only: the reference proxy, Dante,
3proxy, gost, Xray, sing-box, hev\-socks5\-server and shadowsocks\-rust, each
alone and as a middle and exit hop of a chain.
Some proxies silently truncate datagrams larger than their buffer, below the
default
.BR max_datagram ;
set it no higher than the proxy's limit.
See the README for the full list, and what each limitation means in practice.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Resolve a name through the proxy.
.BR dig (1)
queries the resolver it is given itself, so no
.B UDPCHAINS_DNS
is needed:
.PP
.RS
.EX
udpchains \-p 127.0.0.1:1080 dig @9.9.9.9 example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a program that looks up a name through libc, with the lookup also sent
through the proxy:
.PP
.RS
.EX
UDPCHAINS_DNS=9.9.9.9 udpchains \-p 127.0.0.1:1080 nc \-u example.com 53
.EE
.RE
.PP
Send through a chain of two proxies, the second reached through the first:
.PP
.RS
.EX
udpchains \-p 10.0.0.1:1080 \-p 10.1.0.5:1080 dig @9.9.9.9 example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Use the chain in an existing proxychains configuration:
.PP
.RS
.EX
udpchains \-f /etc/proxychains.conf dig @9.9.9.9 example.com
.EE
.RE
.PP
Run a static or Go program in supervisor mode, its lookups sent to 9.9.9.9
through the proxy:
.PP
.RS
.EX
UDPCHAINS_DNS=9.9.9.9 udpchains \-\-supervise \-p 127.0.0.1:1080 ./static\-client
.EE
.RE
.PP
Confirm the traffic really takes the tunnel \- this should report the proxy's
egress address, not the local one:
.PP
.RS
.EX
udpchains \-p 127.0.0.1:1080 dig +short myip.opendns.com @208.67.222.222
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR proxychains (1),
.BR ld.so (8),
.BR seccomp (2),
.BR ptrace (2),
.BR socket (7),
.BR udp (7)
.PP
RFC 1928, section 7.
.SH AUTHORS
Implemented clean\-room from RFC 1928 and the relevant manual pages.
